## INTRODUCTION
Lewis acids efficiently catalyze many different types of organic reactions by withdrawing electron density from functional groups to make them susceptible to nucleophilic attacks. In Diels−Alder reactions, for example, coordination of a dienophile to a Lewis acid significantly decreases its LUMO energy to facilitate its reaction with a diene.4 Catalytic Diels− Alder reactions allow for efficient synthesis of functionalized cyclic compounds that have found widespread use as pharmaceutical ingredients, artificial flavors, fragrances, and agrochemicals.5 Lewis acids are also used to catalyze the formation of epoxides and aromatic ketones and regioselective ring opening of epoxides to yield alcohols.
Early Lewis acid catalysts were typically based on main group or early transition metal halides. Lewis acidity of these metals increases when the halides are replaced with more electron-deficient and less coordinating anions, such as triflate $(-\mathrm{OTf})$ or bis(trifluoromethylsulfonyl)imide $\left(\mathrm{-NTf}_{2}\right)$ .6−8 However, these Lewis acidic metal salts tend to have low solubility in nonpolar organic solvents, are sensitive to moisture, and have short lifetimes during catalysis. In order to overcome these drawbacks, researchers have devoted significant efforts to developing heterogeneous Lewis acid catalysts such as zeolites, metal oxides and resins over the past few decades. Solid acid catalysts can be readily separated from reaction mixtures for reuse and are compatible with flow catalysis,9−11 but they have moderate Lewis acidity, low active site density, and nonuniform active sites.12,13
Metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) have recently emerged as a novel porous material platform for designing homogeneously inaccessible catalysts by taking advantage of their regular framework structures and site isolation effects.14−19 In particular, metal centers in MOF nodes have afforded outstanding single-site solid catalysts with unique electronic properties and steric environments that are not accessible via conventional homogeneous chemistry or heterogenization approaches. Although MOF nodes have been used as Lewis acidic sites to catalyze organic transformations, such as cyclization reactions,20 $C{\mathrm{-}}{\check{\mathrm{H}}}$ iodinations,21 hydrolysis,22,23 and dehydrations,24 using activated substrates, Lewis acidity of these metal sites is significantly lower than the homogeneous benchmark $\l_{S c(\mathrm{{\bar{O}T f})_{3}}}$ .25 We have previously developed two quantitative methods for measuring Lewis acidity of MOF active sites through electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) spectroscopy of MOF-bound superoxide $(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-})$ or fluorescence spectroscopy of MOF-bound $N\cdot$ - methylacridone (NMA).21 Quantitative measurements of Lewis acidity of MOFs can potentially guide the discovery of highly Lewis acidic MOF catalysts for efficient organic synthesis through continuous flow catalysis.21
Several examples of $Z\mathrm{r}$ MOFs have recently been shown to possess relatively strong acidity.20,22,23,26−29 Notably, Yaghi and co-workers developed MOF- ${-808-S\mathrm{O}_{4}}$ as a strong Brönsted acid catalyst for alkene dimerization. The unsaturated $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ nodes of MOF-808 were modified by sulfuric acid to display strong Brönsted acidity (Figure 1).30 By utilizing a distinct strategy, we aim to develop strongly Lewis acidic $\mathrm{z}\mathrm{r}$ MOF catalysts that work through different and complementary reaction mechanisms in this work.31,32 We synthesized Lewis acidic ZrOTf-BTC MOF from $Z\mathbf{r}$ -BTC (MOF-808) through sequential postsynthetic formate removal and triflation of MOF nodes. ZrOTf-BTC is a highly active solid Lewis acid catalyst for a broad range of important organic transformations, including Diels−Alder reaction, epoxide ring-opening reaction, Friedel−Crafts acylation, and alkene hydroalkoxylation reaction. ZrOTf-BTC exhibits higher Lewis acidity than ${\cal S}{\sf c}({\sf O T f})_{3}$ and outperforms ${\mathrm{Sc}}(\mathrm{OTf})_{3}$ in these reactions with higher catalytic activity and longer catalyst lifetime. We further supported ZrOTf-BTC on $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ to afford a composite material as an efficient solid Lewis acid catalyst for continuous flow catalysis.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Synthesis and Characterization of ZrOTf-BTC. We have developed a novel strategy to install metal triflate active sites at MOF nodes through postsynthetic triflation of metal hydroxide groups. $\mathrm{z}\mathbf{r}$ -BTC was synthesized following the literature procedure by a solvothermal reaction of trimesic acid and $\mathrm{ZrOCl}_{2}{\cdot}8\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in a mixture of $^{N,N}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) and formic acid.33 The inorganic node of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -BTC has the composition of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mu_{3}\mathrm{-O})_{4}(\tilde{\mu_{3}}\mathrm{-OH})_{4}(\mathrm{RCO}_{2})_{6}(\mathrm{HCO}_{2})_{6},$ where six formate groups cap all the peripheral sites around the $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ octahedron. $\mathrm{Zr}$ -BTC was subsequently activated with 1
M aqueous HCl at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ to replace the formate groups with six pairs of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -coordinated hydroxide and water groups to afford $Z\mathrm{rOH}$ -BTC with the inorganic node composition of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{RCO}_{2})_{6}[(\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{OH}_{2})]_{6}.$ $\mathrm{ZrOH}$ -BTC was then treated with trimethylsilyl triflate $\left(\mathrm{Me}_{3}\mathrm{SiOTf}\right)$ in benzene at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize8h}$ to afford $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{ZrOTf.}$ BTC. As the oxophilic $\mathrm{Me}_{3}\mathrm{Si}$ group forms stronger bond with the $^{-}\mathrm{OH}$ moiety than with the −OTf moiety, $\mathbf{Me}_{3}\mathrm{SiOTf}$ readily removes $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ from $\mathrm{ZrOH-BTC}$ to generate ZrOTf-BTC with the node composition of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{O})_{4}\overline{{(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{RCO}_{2})_{6}[\mathrm{OTf}]_{6}}}.$ The $\mathrm{\bf{Me}}_{3}\mathrm{SiOH}$ byproduct generated through this activation reacted with excess $\mathbf{Me}_{3}\mathrm{SiOTf}$ to form $(\mathrm{Me}_{3}\mathrm{Si})_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . The amount of $(\mathrm{Me}_{3}\mathrm{Si})_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was quantified to be 1.92 equiv w.r.t. $Z\mathrm{r}$ by ${}^{1}\mathrm{H}~\mathrm{NMR},$ agreeing well with the proposed activation process (Figure S3, Supporting Information).
Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) of ZrOTf-BTC showed a weight loss of $63.8\%$ in the $220-800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ range (Figure S2, Supporting Information), consistent with the expected weight loss of $62.8\%$ for the conversion of $\bar{\mathrm{Zr}_{6}}\bar{(\mu_{3}{-}\mathrm{O})_{4}}\bar{(\mu_{3}{-}}$ $\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}(\mathrm{OTf})_{6}$ to $\left(\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}\right)_{12}$ . Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) studies showed that ZrOTf-BTC remains crystalline and maintains the structure of ZrOH-BTC (Figure 2b). Transmission electron microscope (TEM) images of $\mathrm{ZrOTf-}$ BTC displayed highly crystalline octahedral particles of ${\sim}200$ nm in dimensions. (Figure 2c) The porosity of ZrOTf-BTC was confirmed by ${\bf N}_{2}$ sorption isotherms, with a Brunauer− Emmett−Teller (BET) surface area of $779\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}.$ (Figure 2d) Pore size analysis by nonlinear density functional-theory (NLDFT) showed a uniform pore at ${\sim}16\mathrm{~\AA~}$ that is attributable to the large hexagonal cages of the MOF. The pore size of $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{ZrOTf.}$ BTC is smaller than that of ZrOH-BTC (21 Å) due to the presence of six large triflate groups per SBU (Figure 2e).
Computational and Spectroscopic Studies of Zr-OTf Active Sites. We used a range of computational and spectroscopic techniques, including density functional theory (DFT), extended X-ray absorption fine-structure (EXAFS) and X-ray absorption near-edge spectroscopy (XANES), to elucidate the $Z\mathrm{r}$ coordination environment in ZrOTf-BTC. The first question we attempted to address is whether triflate groups are incorporated into the MOF. We performed sulfur K-edge XANES analysis to prove the presence of triflate groups. The sulfur centers in ZrOTf-BTC displayed significant edge-step, with the K-edge energy of at $2479.8\ \mathrm{eV}.$ This energy is much higher than that of sodium thiosulfate $(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3})$ standard and matched well to the edge energy of ${\mathrm{sc}}({\mathrm{OTf}})_{3},$ indicating the presence of triflate groups in ZrOTf-BTC (Figure 3b).
The next question we attempted to address is how triflate groups are coordinated to $\mathrm{z}\mathrm{r}$ centers. We envisioned two possible modes of triflate coordination (Figure 3a): OTf bridging two proximal $Z\mathbf{r}$ centers (the $\mu_{2}$ -model) or OTf chelation to a single $\mathrm{Zr}$ center (the $\eta^{2}$ -model). Both of these two coordination modes are commonly observed in the crystal structures of molecular metal-OTf complexes. Both structures were optimized using DFT at the B3LYP level of theory, and their free energies were calculated to compare their relative stability. The optimized structure for the $\mu_{2}$ -model showed an average $\mathrm{Zr-O}^{\mathrm{{\hat{O}}T f}}$ bond distance of $2.269\mathring{\mathrm{A}},$ which is similar to reported ${\bf Z}{\bf r}{-}{\bf O}^{\mathrm{OTf}}$ bond distances in molecular $\mathrm{Zr}_{2}(\mathrm{OTf})$ complexes.34,35 The optimized structure for the $\eta^{2}$ -model showed an average $\mathrm{Zr{-}O^{\mathrm{{oTf}}}}$ bond distance of $2.299\mathring{\mathrm{A}},$ which is longer than that of the $\mu_{2}$ model by $0.030\mathring{\mathrm{A}}.$ Bond distance analysis thus showed stronger bonding between $\mu_{2}$ -triflates and
$Z\mathbf{r}$ centers. Free energy calculations also indicated the $\mu_{2}$ model is thermodynamically more stable, with 24.85 kcal· $\mathrm{mol^{-1}}$ lower free energy than the $\eta^{2}$ -model per six $\mathrm{Zr}$ centers. The DFT optimized $\mu_{2}$ model fitted well to the $\mathrm{Zr}$ k-edge EXAFS spectrum of ZrOTf-BTC, with an R-factor of 0.007 (Figure 3c, Table S2, Supporting Information). The fitted $Z\mathbf{r}.$ - OTf bond distances have an average value of $2.20\mathring{\mathrm{A}},$ similar to that calculated by DFT $(2.269\mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ . The $\boldsymbol{z}_{\mathrm{rO}}$ Tf-BTC spectrum was also fitted with the DFT optimized $\eta^{2}$ model, but the fitting afforded significant shortening of the $\boldsymbol{{\mathrm{Zr}}}\boldsymbol{\cdot}\boldsymbol{\eta}^{2}$ -OTf bond distance by $0.090{\overset{\sim}{\mathrm{A}}}$ to $2.209\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ when compared to the DFT optimized $\mathrm{\partial^{2r-O^{OTf}}}$ distance (Figure S5, Table S3, Supporting Information). EXAFS studies thus favor the $\mu_{2}$ coordination mode as well.
Quantification of ZrOTf-BTC Lewis Acidity by Spectroscopic Methods. We then quantified the Lewis acidity of ZrOTf-BTC using two spectroscopic methods that were recently developed, including electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) spectroscopy of MOF-bound superoxides $(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-})$ and fluorescence spectroscopy of MOF-bound $N$ -methylacridone (NMA).21 ZrOTf-BTC was shown to be more Lewis acidic than ZrOH-BTC and the previously reported $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -fBDC based on the Lewis acidity measurement using both techniques.
NMA fluorescence test with homogeneous ${\mathrm{Sc}}(\mathrm{OTf})_{3}$ also gave a ΔE of $0.98\mathrm{~\eV}$ (Figure S8, Supporting Information). Triflation of metal-hydroxides thus provides an effective strategy to enhance MOF Lewis acidity and to generate porous solid acids with Lewis acidity comparable to ${\mathrm{Sc}}(\mathrm{OTf})_{3}$ .
Synthesis and Characterization of $\mathsf{Z r O T f-B T C}@\mathsf{S i O}_{2}.$ Although Lewis acids have broad applications in organic synthesis, little progress has been made in immobilizing Lewis acids for flow synthesis.39,40 $\mathrm{BF}_{3}$ and ${\mathrm{Sc}}(\mathrm{OTf})_{3}$ are commonly used to catalyze organic transformations, but they are difficult to incorporate into heterogeneous supports without sacrificing acidity.41,42 As a result, most flow processes used Lewis acids in stoichiometry, causing significant waste of catalysts and quenching reagents and corrosion of reaction vessels. Owing to their tunable structures and high porosity, MOFs and related organic−inorganic hybrid materials represent an attractive class of solid catalysts for flow catalysis.43−46 We sought to utilize ZrOTf-BTC as an effective fixed-bed Lewis acid catalyst to achieve flow catalysis of a broad scope of reactions.
Due to the tight packing of MOF particles and the slow substrate diffusion rate through the nanosized MOF channels, packing pure MOFs into a column can lead to very high column pressure. Growing MOFs into much larger sizes or simply mix the MOF with inert filler can reduce the column pressure, but organic substrates cannot easily access the interiors of such MOF particles due to the increased diffusion barrier. To overcome this limitation, we have directly grown $\boldsymbol{z}_{\mathrm{rO}}$ Tf-BTC on silica to afford the $\mathrm{ZrOTf-BTC}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ composite material.47−49 $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Zr-BTC}_{(\theta\mathrm{SiO}_{2})}$ was first prepared analogously to $Z\mathrm{r}$ -BTC except with the addition of 30 weight equiv of $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ . Surface silanol groups on $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ served as the nucleation sites for the formation of octahedron-shaped $Z\mathbf{r}.$ - BTC particles of an average dimension of ${\sim}50~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S9, Supporting Information). Due to enhanced nucleation on silica surface, the MOF particles in the composite material are smaller than pure MOF particles and are densely packed on silica.
$\mathrm{Zr{-BTC}}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ was treated with 1 M HCl to remove formate capping groups and then triflated with $\mathbf{Me}_{3}\mathrm{SiOTf}$ to form $\mathrm{ZrOTf-BTC}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ as an off-white powder. TEM imaging showed that $\mathrm{ZrOTf-BTC}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ possessed core−shell structures with the MOF particles displaying high crystallinity and octahedral morphology (Figure 5c, 5d). SEM imaging also showed a dense coating of ZrOTf-BTC particles on the silica surface (Figure S13, Supporting Information). $\mathrm{ZrOTf-BTC}\textcircled{a}$ $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ exhibited the same PXRD pattern as $\mathrm{Zr}$ -BTC, indicating that the MOF in the composite maintained the identical structure to pristine MOF (Figure 5b). ${\bf N}_{2}$ sorption isotherms of $\mathrm{ZrOTf-BTC}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ gave a BET surface area of $321\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}.$ The composite material showed a sharp $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ sorption at $P/P_{0}<$ 0.2, which can be attributed to micropore filling of $Z\mathrm{rOTf}.$ BTC, and a broad adsorption with hysteresis at $P/P_{0}=0.2\mathrm{-}$ 0.9, which is attributable to mesopore sorption by silica particles. Pore size analysis by NL-DFT indicated the presence of a uniform micropore at $1.6~\mathrm{nm}$ and a broad range of larger pores at $2{-}10~\mathrm{\nm}$ (Figure 5e, 5f). The $Z\mathbf{r}$ coordination environment was studied by EXAFS. By using the $\mu_{2}$ -triflate and $Z\mathrm{r}$ coordination sphere of $\mathrm{Zr(OT\bar{f})}_{2}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{O})_{2}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}$ $\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{RCO}_{2})_{2},$ the $Z\mathbf{r}$ K-edge EXAFS data of ZrOTf-BTC$\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ was well fitted with an average $Z\mathrm{r}$ -OTf bond distance of 2.217 $\mathring\mathrm{A}$ (Figure ${\mathfrak{s g}},$ Table S4, Supporting Information). The $Z\mathbf{r}$ active sites in $\mathrm{ZrOTf-BTC}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ thus adopts identical coordination environment as those in ZrOTf-BTC. The $\mathrm{ZrOTf-BTC}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ powder was slurry-packed with $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ into a stainless-steel column for flow catalysis studies. The amount of ZrOTf-BTC was quantified to be $40\ \mu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{Zr}$ per gram by $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR analysis of the digested material.
ZrOTf-BTC Catalyzed Diels−Alder Reactions. The Diels−Alder reaction is a very efficient strategy for constructing six-membered ring structures with regioselectivity and stereoselectivity.5,50 This reaction generally requires the addition of an acid catalyst to reduce reaction temperature and time. Unlike homogeneous Lewis acidic metal complexes, MOF-based Lewis acids offer significant advantages including easy separation from reaction mixtures and catalyst recovery/ reuse. Moreover, uniform active sites of MOF catalysts generally afford higher reaction selectivity. For the reaction of 1,4-benzoquinone with cyclohexa-1,3-diene, the cyclization product was obtained in quantitative yield within $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ at only $\bar{1}.0\ \mathrm{mol}\ \%$ ZrOTf-BTC loading under room temperature. No obvious change was observed in the MOF crystallinity by PXRD (Figure S17, Supporting Information), whereas only minimum $Z\mathbf{r}$ $(0.13\%)$ leached into the supernatant after each reaction run by ICP-MS analysis. When the catalyst loading was reduced to $0.1\ \mathrm{\mol}\ \%$ , the cyclization product was obtained in $76\%$ yield in $^\mathrm{10h,}$ affording a TON of 760. The Diels−Alder reaction is significantly more challenging with electron-rich dienophiles such as $\alpha{,}\beta$ -unsaturated ketones and aldehydes due to their much higher LUMO energy. Impressively, at $5.0\ \mathrm{\mol}\ \%$ catalyst loading, ZrOTf-BTC catalyzed the cyclization of a series of challenging dienophiles, including 3-buten-2-one, methacrolein and crotonaldehyde, with both cyclohexa-1,3-diene and 2,3-dimethylbuta-1,3-diene in high yields (Table 1).
The heterogeneity of ZrOTf-BTC in the Diels−Alder reaction was confirmed by the “hot filtration” test. After a reaction run, the MOF and supernatant were separated and separately used as catalyst for another reaction run. While the catalytic activity was maintained in the solid MOF, no activity was observed for the supernatant. This “hot filtration” test rules out the possibility of leached $Z\mathbf{r}$ salts or soluble Brönsted acids contributing to the catalytic reactivity (Figure S18, Supporting Information).
We then tested the Diels−Alder reaction using the $\mathrm{\Delta}Z\mathrm{r}{\mathrm{OTf-}}$ $\mathbf{BTC}\ @\mathbf{SiO}_{2}$ packed column as a continuous flow catalyst. A $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ solution of benzoquinone (limiting reagent) and cyclohexa-1,3-diene $\left(0.5\mathrm{~M}\right)^{}$ in a molar ratio of 1:1.6 was flowed through the column at a rate of $10\ \mathrm{mL}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ to achieve complete conversion of benzoquinone and to produce the cyclization product in quantitative yield. The turnover frequency (TOF) of the flow process was calculated to be $10\bar{0}\mathrm{{h}^{-1}}$ . The flow reaction was run for a TON of 1700 in $17\mathrm{h}$ without a drop in the reaction yield (Figure 6).
ZrOTf-BTC Catalyzed Epoxide Ring-Opening Amination. Epoxides are an important class of industrial chemicals for conversion into a broad range of commodity and fine chemicals.51,52 For instance, the nucleophilic ring-opening with amines affords $\beta\mathrm{.}$ -amino alcohols, which are useful intermediates for organic synthesis.40,53 Several homogeneous catalysts, including metal triflates and metal halides have been used for this reaction.54−57 However, very few heterogeneous catalysts have been tested for this reaction to achieve catalyst reuse and flow catalysis. ZrOTf-BTC is a very active catalyst for epoxide ring-opening with anilines. At 5.0 mol $\%$ ZrOTf-BTC loading, many different epoxides, including styrene oxide, cyclohexene oxide and cyclopentene oxide, reacted with aniline to form corresponding amino alcohols (Table 2) without heating. A broad range of aniline derivatives, including electron-deficient aniline (4-chloroaniline) and electron-rich aniline (2,4,6- trimethylaniline), and sterically hindered secondary anilines (N-methylaniline) can all be used for the ring-opening of epoxides to afford amino alcohols in high yields (Table 2).
The epoxide ring-opening reaction also worked well under flow conditions. Styrene oxide (limiting reagent) and aniline solution in $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ $\left(0.5\mathrm{~M}\right)$ were flowed through $\mathrm{\Delta}Z\mathrm{r}{\mathrm{OTf-}}$ ${\mathrm{BTC@SiO}}_{2}$ column at a rate of $60\ \mathrm{mL}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ to form the amino alcohol product in $93\%$ yield. High yields $(83-93\%)$ of product were consistently obtained in 30 runs to afford a total TON of $>2700$ in $30\mathrm{~h~}$ (Figure 7).
ZrOTf-BTC Catalyzed Friedel−Crafts Acylation Reactions. As one of the most convenient and useful strategies for the construction of aryl ketone moieties in a wide range of pharmaceuticals and agricultural chemicals, Friedel−Crafts acylation has drawn continuous research interests.58 Conventional Lewis acids for Friedel−Crafts acylation include traditional metal halides (e.g., $\mathrm{ZnCl}_{2},$ $\mathrm{AlCl}_{3},\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}\rangle$ and metal triflates (e.g., ${\mathrm{Sc}}(\mathrm{OTf})_{3},$ $\mathrm{Hf(OTf)_{4}},$ .59−61 However, due to the coordination between the Lewis acid and the produced aromatic ketone, stoichiometric amount of metal halides are usually required in these reactions. Furthermore, since the workup procedure typically requires aqueous treatment, the recovery of these homogeneous Lewis acidic metal salts and the generation of large amounts of wastes are long-standing challenges. As a result, significant efforts have been devoted to the development of heterogeneous Lewis acid catalyst for Friedel−Crafts acylation. Modified zeolites,62,63 metal oxides,64 heteropoly acids,65 and hybrid materials66,67 have displayed moderate to good activity in Friedel−Crafts acylation.
At $1.0\mathrm{\mol}\mathrm{\}\%$ of loading, ZrOTf-BTC catalyzed Friedel− Crafts acylation between 2-methoxynaphthalene and neat acetic anhydride in $^\mathrm{~2~h~}$ at room temperature to afford 1- acetyl-2-methoxynaphthalene in $98\%$ isolated yield. This level of activity is much higher than those of most Lewis acid catalysts including homogeneous metal triflates and solid acid catalysts. Several substituted arenes, including anisole, dimethoxybenzene, mesitylene, and benzofuran, underwent acylation in the presence of $1{-}5\ \mathrm{mol}\ \%$ of $\mathrm{ZrOTf-BTC}$ at room temperature to afford desired products in $63\mathrm{-}83\%$ yields (Table 3). Furthermore, $5.0\ \mathrm{\mol}\ \%$ ZrOTf-BTC catalyzed Friedel−Crafts acylation with benzoic anhydride in $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ to afford 1-benzoyl-2-methoxynaphthalene in $81\%$ yield.
The catalytic performance of Friedel−Crafts acylation was also tested in the continuous flow mode. When a solution of 2- methoxynaphthalene $(0.05\mathrm{~M})$ in $\mathrm{Ac}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ (1:4, $\mathbf{v}/\mathbf{v})$ was flowed through $\mathrm{ZrOTf-BTC}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ column at a rate of 30 $\mathrm{\mL\cdoth^{-1}}$ , 1-acetyl-2-methoxynaphthalene was formed as the desired product in excellent yields $(85-99\%)$ in the first 5 runs ( $\mathbf{\sigma}_{5\mathrm{mL}}$ solution per run). The yield dropped to $65\%$ in the six run but the catalytic performance was restored by simply washing the column with a $\mathrm{CH_{3}C N/C H_{2}C l_{2}}$ $(1{:}9,\ \mathbf{v}/\mathbf{v})$
