ZrOTf-BTC Catalyzed Alkene Hydroalkoxylation Reactions. ZrOTf-BTC is also a highly active catalyst for alkene hydroalkoxylation reactions. Oxygen-containing cyclic compounds are abundant in polyether antibiotics and other biologically active natural products as well as in chemical feedstocks.68,69 The addition of alcohols across $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C}}={\mathrm{C}}$ bonds is the most straightforward synthetic route to cyclic ethers.70,71 This reaction pathway is widely adopted by biological systems to synthesize cyclic ether-containing natural products.72,73 Some homogeneous catalysts are effective for this reaction, but many of them require activated alkenes (e.g., alkenes, dienes, and Michael acceptors).74−77 We report here the first example of MOF-catalyzed alkene hydroalkoxylation using highly acidic ZrOTf-BTC.
aReaction conditions: epoxides (1 equiv, $1.0\mathrm{\mmol}\dot{}$ ), anilines (1.2 equiv, $1.2\ \mathrm{mmol}\$ ), ZrOTf-BTC $\left(1.0{-}5.0\ \mathrm{mol}\ \%\right)$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ $\mathrm{\cdot}4.0\ \mathrm{mL}$ , $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ Reaction yields were determined by $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR using mesitylene as internal standard.
For the cyclization of 4-penten-1-ol, $2.0\ \mathrm{mol}\ \%$ of $\operatorname{ZrOTf-}$ BTC afforded 2-methyltetrahydrofuran in quantitative yield after heating at $135^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under inert atmosphere for $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ (Table 4). 5-Hexen-1-ol was also hydroalkoxylated in $91\%$ yield at 4.0 mol $\%$ ZrOTf-BTC to afford 2-methyltetrahydropyran and 2- ethyltetrahydrofuran in a 3:1 ratio. Besides aliphatic alcohols, alkene substrates containing phenol groups such as 2- allylphenol and 2-allyl-6-methylphenol were also catalytically cyclized with ZrOTf-BTC at a lower temperature of $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Alkene-containing carboxylic acids such as pent-4-enoic acid and hex-5-enoic acid were also hydroalkoxylated to form corresponding lactones in excellent yields $(91-95\%)$ at 1.0 mol $\%$ catalyst loading.
To further demonstrate the advantage of ZrOTf-BTC over conventional ${\mathrm{Sc}}(\mathrm{OTf})_{3}$ in catalyzing alkene hydroalkoxylation reactions, we conducted catalytic hydroalkoxylation of pent-4- enoic acid at $0.2\mathrm{\mol}\mathrm{\}\%$ loading of ZrOTf-BTC or ${\cal S}{\sf c}({\sf O T f})_{3}$ . As shown in Figure 9, ZrOTf-BTC catalyzed the hydroalkoxylation of pent-4-enoic acid in octane to afford $90\%$ of $\gamma-$ valerolactone in $^{72\mathrm{~h},}$ while very little product was detected in the presence of ${\mathrm{Sc}}(\mathrm{OTf})_{3}$ under the same condition. The lack of catalytic activity of ${\mathrm{Sc}}(\mathrm{OTf})_{3}$ is likely due to its poor aReaction conditions: arene (1 equiv, $1.0\ \mathrm{mmol}\cdot$ ), acetic anhydride $\mathrm{\Omega}^{\mathrm{\prime1.0~mL,}}$ excess) or benzoic anhydride (3 equiv, $3.0\ \mathrm{mmol},$ in $2.0~\mathrm{mL}$ $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2},$ ), ZrOTf-BTC $\left(1.0{-}5.0\ \mathrm{mol}\ \%\right)$ ), $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Isolated yields are listed.
solubility in nonpolar octane. Changing the solvent to polar $\mathrm{MeNO}_{2}$ afforded 2-methyltetrahydrofuran in $20\%$ yield at 0.2 mol $\%$ ${\mathrm{Sc}}(\mathrm{OTf})_{3}$ in $s\mathrm{~h~}$ . However, no further substrate conversion was observed beyond $s\ \mathrm{h},$ suggesting the deactivation of $\csc(\mathrm{OTf})_{3}$ in polar solvent at elevated temperature. ZrOTf-BTC thus shows much longer lifetime than ${\mathrm{Sc}}(\mathrm{OTf})_{3}$ in alkene hydroalkoxylation. Additionally, as a heterogeneous catalyst, ZrOTf-BTC was easily recovered from the reaction mixture via centrifugation and reused at least 5 times without significant activity decrease (Figure S28, Supporting Information). ZrOTf-BTC thus shows significant advantage over traditional homogeneous Lewis acid catalysts in catalytic activity, lifetime, and catalyst recovery and reuse.
## CONCLUSION
We have designed a strongly Lewis acidic MOF, ZrOTf-BTC, through two-step SBU transformations of MOF-808. The Lewis acidity of the $Z\mathrm{r}$ -triflate active site was quantified through spectroscopic methods to be comparable to the homogeneous benchmark ${\mathrm{Sc}}(\mathrm{OTf})_{3}$ . ZrOTf-BTC was shown to be a highly active solid Lewis acid catalyst for a broad range aReaction conditions: substrate $\left(1.0\mathrm{\mmol}\right)^{\cdot}$ ), ZrOTf-BTC (1.0−4.0 mol $\%$ ), octane $\left(2.0\mathrm{\mL}\right)$ , $100{-}135^{\circ}\mathrm{C},.$ $^{18\ \mathrm{h},}$ inert atmosphere. Reaction yields were determined by $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR using mesitylene as internal standard.
of organic transformations, including Diels−Alder reaction, epoxide ring-opening reaction, Friedel−Crafts acylation, and alkene hydroalkoxylation reactions. The catalytic performance of $\mathrm{ZrOTf-BTC}$ is superior over ${\cal S}{\sf c}({\sf O T f})_{3}$ in terms of catalyst activity, lifetime, and reusability. We have further developed $\mathrm{ZrOTf-BTC}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ composite for continuous flow catalysis. $\mathrm{ZrOTf-BTC}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ displayed exceptionally high turnover numbers (TONs) of 1600 for Diels−Alder reaction, 2700 for epoxide ring-opening reaction, and 326 for Friedel−Crafts acylation under flow conditions. We have thus demonstrated the creation of strongly Lewis acidic sites in MOFs via triflation and utility of $\mathrm{MOF}@{\mathrm{SiO}_{2}}$ composite in continuous flow catalysis of important organic transformations.
